{
  "_comment": [
    "Normative dHash test vectors: one 9x8 greyscale grid per entry, row-major, and the",
    "64-bit difference hash guard_schema::visual::IconHash::from_grid_9x8 produces for it.",
    "",
    "This file exists because the hash has two implementations in two languages -- Rust in",
    "guard-schema and Kotlin in the Android companion's AppFace -- and the comparison is",
    "meaningless unless they agree bit for bit. AppFace.kt says so in its own header: 'the",
    "algorithm is normative ... reimplemented here rather than shared'. Until now nothing",
    "checked that. Both sides now assert against this file, so a drift fails a test instead",
    "of quietly turning every icon comparison into noise.",
    "",
    "Every vector is non-degenerate (8..56 set bits) because both implementations refuse a",
    "degenerate hash, so a degenerate vector would test the refusal and not the algorithm.",
    "",
    "Bit order is normative too: row 0's leftmost comparison is the most significant bit, and",
    "a bit is set when the LEFT sample is strictly brighter."
